PART 1 – Understanding Git Stash
1. What is Git Stash?
Start with a real-world scenario.

Suppose a developer is working on a bakery website.

The developer is currently working on the payment page.

They have modified three files:
fairy-bake/
│
├── index.html
├── payment.html
└── style.css

Suddenly, the project manager says:

"Stop working on the payment page. There is an urgent bug in the homepage. Fix it immediately."

Now the developer has a problem.

Their payment-page changes are incomplete.

They don't want to commit incomplete code.

They also don't want to lose their modifications.

What should they do?

Answer: Git Stash.
Definition
Git Stash temporarily saves changes from your working directory and staging area, allowing you to switch to another task without creating a commit.

Think of Stash as a temporary storage area for unfinished work.
Real-life analogy
Imagine you are writing an assignment.

Suddenly, your teacher asks you to complete another urgent assignment.

You put your current assignment inside a folder, complete the urgent task and later resume your original assignment.

Git Stash works similarly.
Git Stash workflow
Developer working on Feature A
             |
             |
       Incomplete Changes
             |
             v
          git stash
             |
             v
     Changes temporarily saved
             |
             v
       Switch to Feature B
             |
             v
        Complete urgent work
             |
             v
       Return to Feature A
             |
             v
       git stash pop
             |
             v
     Continue previous work

     Practical 1 – Understanding Git Stash
Step 1. Create a project
mkdir git-stash-lab
cd git-stash-lab

git init

Step 2. Create a file
echo "Welcome to The Fairy Bake" > index.html

Stage and commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Initial bakery website"

Step 3. Modify the file
echo "Adding payment functionality" >> index.html

Check:
git status

You will see that the file has been modified.
Step 4. Save the changes using Stash
git stash

Expected output will indicate that the working directory and index state have been saved.

Check:
git status

The working directory should now be clean.

Explain: The uncommitted modification has been temporarily saved in Git's stash.
Step 5. View the stash
git stash list

Example:
stash@{0}: WIP on main: abc1234 Initial bakery website

Step 6. Restore the changes
git stash pop

Check:
git status

Your previous modifications should be restored.

Important: git stash pop restores the latest stash and removes it from the stash list if the operation succeeds.
PART 2 – Important Git Stash Commands
Students must understand the difference between these commands.
2. Git Stash List
git stash list

Displays all saved stashes.
3. Git Stash Apply
git stash apply

Restores the latest stash but keeps it in the stash list.
4. Git Stash Pop
git stash pop

Restores the latest stash and removes it from the stash list if successful.
5. Git Stash Drop
git stash drop

Deletes the latest stash entry.
6. Git Stash Clear
git stash clear

Deletes all stash entries.

Warning: Stash clear is destructive. Do not demonstrate it on a student's important repository.
7. Stashing with a message
git stash push -m "Payment page incomplete"

This makes it easier to identify saved work.
8. Stashing untracked files
By default, Git Stash does not include untracked files.

To include them:
git stash -u

To include ignored files as well:
git stash -a

Be careful with -a, particularly when ignored files contain credentials or large generated files.
Comparison table
Command	Purpose
git stash	Save tracked changes
git stash -u	Save tracked and untracked changes
git stash list	List stashes
git stash apply	Restore without removing stash
git stash pop	Restore and remove stash after success
git stash drop	Delete one stash
git stash clear	Delete all stashes
git stash show -p	Display saved changes


Classroom question
A developer wants to restore saved changes but keep a copy in the stash.

Which command should they use?

Answer:
git stash apply

PART 3 – Understanding Git Cherry-Pick
9. What is Git Cherry-Pick?
This is an extremely useful command.

Imagine that your development team has two branches:
main
feature-payment

The developer working on feature-payment has completed three commits:
1. Added payment page.
2. Added payment validation.
3. Fixed a small CSS issue.
Now the project manager wants only the payment validation fix on the main branch.

Should we merge the entire feature branch?

No. We can use Git Cherry-Pick.
Definition
Git Cherry-Pick applies the changes introduced by an existing commit onto the current branch, creating a new commit.
Real-life analogy
Imagine a student has three assignments:
- Assignment A.
- Assignment B.
- Assignment C.
The teacher asks the student to submit only Assignment B.

The student selects only that particular assignment.

Cherry-picking works similarly.
Visual explanation
main:

A ---- B ---- C


feature:

A ---- B ---- D ---- E ---- F

Suppose we want only commit E on main.

After Cherry-Pick:
main:

A ---- B ---- C ---- E'


feature:

A ---- B ---- D ---- E ---- F

Notice that E' is a new commit containing the changes from E.

Its commit ID will be different from the original E.
Practical 2 – Git Cherry-Pick
Step 1. Create a repository
mkdir cherry-pick-lab
cd cherry-pick-lab

git init

Step 2. Create the initial commit
echo "Bakery Website" > index.html

git add .
git commit -m "Initial website"

Step 3. Create a feature branch
git switch -c feature-payment

Step 4. Create three commits
First:
echo "Payment Page" > payment.html

git add .
git commit -m "Added payment page"

Second:
echo "Payment Validation" > validation.txt

git add .
git commit -m "Added payment validation"

Third:
echo "Payment Styling" > payment.css

git add .
git commit -m "Added payment styling"

Step 5. View history
git log --oneline

Copy the commit ID for:
Added payment validation

Step 6. Switch to main
git switch main

Step 7. Cherry-pick the required commit
git cherry-pick <commit-id>

Step 8. Verify
git log --oneline

Check:
ls

You should see:
index.html
validation.txt

The payment validation change has been applied to main without merging the entire feature branch.
Important commands
git cherry-pick <commit-id>

Apply a specific commit.
git cherry-pick --continue

Continue after resolving a conflict.
git cherry-pick --abort

Cancel an in-progress cherry-pick.

Important: Cherry-pick can introduce duplicate or overlapping changes if the same work is later merged through another route. Explain why teams coordinate selective commits carefully.
PART 4 – Understanding Git Tags
10. What is a Git Tag?
Imagine your bakery application has reached its first stable version.

The company wants to release it as:
Version 1.0

Later, it releases:
Version 2.0

How can developers mark important versions of their project?

They use Git Tags.
Definition
A Git Tag is a named reference to a specific Git object, commonly a commit.

Tags are frequently used to identify software releases.
Real-life analogy
Think of a textbook.

The publisher releases:
- Edition 1.
- Edition 2.
- Edition 3.
Each edition represents an important version.

Git Tags help identify important project versions.
Visual explanation

A ---- B ---- C ---- D ---- E
          |           |
         v1.0        v2.0

Here:
- v1.0 identifies commit C.
- v2.0 identifies commit E.
11. Types of Git Tags
There are two important types.
A. Lightweight Tag
A lightweight tag is simply a reference to a commit.

Command:
git tag v1.0

It does not store the full tag annotation and metadata.
B. Annotated Tag
An annotated tag stores additional information, including:
- Tag name.
- Tagger name.
- Date.
- Tag message.
- Tag object metadata.
Command:
git tag -a v1.0 -m "Version 1.0 release"

Comparison
Lightweight Tag	Annotated Tag
Simple reference	Full tag object
No annotation message	Supports a message
Minimal metadata	Stores tagger information and date
Useful for temporary/private markers	Preferred for formal releases


Teaching recommendation: Use annotated tags for formal software releases.
Practical 3 – Creating Git Tags
Create a project:
mkdir git-tag-lab
cd git-tag-lab

git init

Create a file:
echo "The Fairy Bake Version 1" > index.html

Commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Initial bakery release"

Create an annotated tag:
git tag -a v1.0 -m "First stable release"

Check:
git tag

Expected:
v1.0

Inspect:
git show v1.0

Now modify the application:
echo "Added product section" >> index.html

Commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Added product section"

Create another tag:
git tag -a v2.0 -m "Second release"

Check:
git tag

You should see:
v1.0
v2.0

Additional Tag Commands
List tags:
git tag

Create a lightweight tag:
git tag v3.0

Tag a specific commit:
git tag -a v1.5 <commit-id> -m "Maintenance release"

Delete a local tag:
git tag -d v1.0

Push one tag to GitHub:
git push origin v2.0

Push all local tags:
git push origin --tags

Important: A normal git push does not automatically push all local tags.
PART 5 – Understanding GitHub Releases
12. What is a GitHub Release?
A Git tag identifies a point in Git history.

A GitHub Release is a GitHub feature that packages and presents a particular version of a project for users.

For example:
The Fairy Bake

Releases:

v1.0 – Initial Release
v2.0 – Added Product Section
v3.0 – Added Payment Functionality

Difference between Tag and Release
Git Tag	GitHub Release
Git reference	GitHub release-management feature
Identifies a commit	Presents a project version
Can be created locally	Managed on GitHub
Can be pushed to remote	Can include release notes and downloadable assets


Practical 4 – Create a GitHub Release
1. Push your project to GitHub.
2. Push the tag:
git push origin v1.0

3. Open the GitHub repository.
4. Navigate to Releases.
5. Select Draft a new release.
6. Choose the tag v1.0.
7. Enter release title:
The Fairy Bake Version 1.0

8. Add release notes.
9. Publish the release.
This is how software teams manage application versions.
PART 6 – Understanding Advanced Git Rebase
Students already learned basic rebase in Session 4.

Now we will go deeper.
13. What is Git Rebase?
Git Rebase moves or replays, commits from one branch onto another base commit.

Suppose:
          C ---- D       main
         /
A ---- B
         \
          E ---- F       feature

Main has moved forward.

Feature was created earlier.

We want the feature branch to incorporate the latest main changes while keeping a linear history.

Execute:
git switch feature
git rebase main

Conceptually, Git replays E and F on top of D.
A ---- B ---- C ---- D ---- E' ---- F'

Notice:
- Original commits E and F are not simply moved.
- Git creates new commits E' and F'.
- Their commit IDs change.
- The resulting history is linear.
Why do developers use Rebase?
1. Maintain a cleaner history.
2. Incorporate recent changes from main.
3. Avoid unnecessary merge commits.
4. Prepare feature branches before Pull Requests.
Merge vs Rebase
Git Merge	Git Rebase
Combines branch histories	Replays commits
Can create merge commits	Usually creates linear history
Preserves original commits	Replayed commits receive new IDs
Good for integrating shared branches	Useful for updating private feature branches


Important rule: Never casually rebase shared branches. It rewrites commit history and can disrupt collaborators.
PART 7 – Understanding Interactive Rebase
14. What is Interactive Rebase?
This is a more advanced Git feature.

Suppose a developer creates five commits:
Added homepage
Fixed typo
Updated homepage
Fixed CSS
Added navigation

The project manager asks the developer to clean up the commit history before submitting a Pull Request.

Interactive Rebase allows developers to:
- Reorder commits.
- Change commit messages.
- Combine commits.
- Edit commits.
- Drop commits.
Command:
git rebase -i HEAD~5

This opens an editor containing the last five commits.

You may see:
pick a123456 Added homepage
pick b234567 Fixed typo
pick c345678 Updated homepage
pick d456789 Fixed CSS
pick e567890 Added navigation

Important Interactive Rebase commands
Command	Meaning
pick	Keep the commit
reword	Change the commit message
edit	Stop and allow modification
squash	Combine commit with previous commit
fixup	Combine changes and discard the commit message
drop	Remove the commit


Practical demonstration
Create five commits in a disposable repository.

Then execute:
git rebase -i HEAD~5

Change the editor instructions to:
pick a123456 Added homepage
squash b234567 Fixed typo
pick c345678 Updated homepage
fixup d456789 Fixed CSS
pick e567890 Added navigation

Save and close the editor.

Git will combine the selected commits.

Check:
git log --oneline

You should see fewer commits.

Warning: Interactive rebase rewrites history. Use it on your own unshared commits, not casually on commits already used by other developers.
PART 8 – Understanding Git Bisect
15. What is Git Bisect?
Practical 5 – Find the Buggy Commit
Create a repository:
mkdir git-bisect-lab
cd git-bisect-lab

git init

Create the first version:
echo "Application working correctly" > app.txt

git add .
git commit -m "Working version"

Create additional commits.
echo "Feature 1" >> app.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Added feature 1"

echo "Feature 2" >> app.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Added feature 2"

Now introduce a bug:
echo "BUG INTRODUCED" >> app.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Introduced bug"

Create another commit:
echo "Feature 3" >> app.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Added feature 3"

Step 1. Start Bisect
git bisect start

Step 2. Mark the current version as bad
git bisect bad

Step 3. Identify a known good commit
git log --oneline

Copy the first working commit ID.

Execute:
git bisect good <good-commit-id>

Git will select a commit for testing.
Step 4. Test the selected version
Inspect:
cat app.txt

If the application works:
git bisect good

If the application contains the bug:
git bisect bad

Repeat until Git identifies the first bad commit.
Step 5. Finish Bisect
git bisect reset

This returns the repository to the previous checkout state.

Teaching point: Git Bisect is particularly useful in large repositories where bugs are introduced somewhere in a long commit history.
PART 9 – Understanding Git Blame
16. What is Git Blame?
Imagine you are debugging a project.

You find this line:
payment_status = "SUCCESS"


You want to know:
- Who introduced this line?
- Which commit introduced it?
- When was it changed?
Use:
git blame filename

Git Blame displays line-by-line information about the last commit that modified each line.
Practical 6 – Git Blame
Create a file:
nano app.py

Add:
name = "The Fairy Bake"location = "Delhi"payment_status = "PENDING"


Commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Added application configuration"

Modify one line:
payment_status = "SUCCESS"


Commit again:
git add .
git commit -m "Updated payment status"

Now execute:
git blame app.py

You will see information such as:
<commit-id> (Student Name 2026-11-18) name = "The Fairy Bake"
<commit-id> (Student Name 2026-11-18) location = "Delhi"
<commit-id> (Student Name 2026-11-18) payment_status = "SUCCESS"

The actual output depends on the author and commit history.
Important point
Git Blame does not mean blaming a developer for mistakes.

It is used to understand the history and origin of particular lines of code.

PART 10 – Understanding Git Clean
17. What is Git Clean?
Suppose your project contains temporary files.
project/
│
├── index.html
├── app.py
├── temp.txt
├── debug.log
└── backup/

You do not want to commit these temporary files.

Git Clean removes untracked files and directories.
Important commands
First preview what would be removed:
git clean -n

Remove untracked files:
git clean -f

Remove untracked files and directories:
git clean -fd

Remove ignored files as well:
git clean -fdX

Remove all untracked and ignored files:
git clean -fdx

Very important: Git Clean is destructive.

Always use:
git clean -n

before executing the actual deletion command.

It does not normally remove tracked files.
PART 11 – Understanding Git Hooks
18. What are Git Hooks?
This is especially important for DevSecOps.

Imagine your company wants to ensure that every developer follows certain rules.

For example:
- No commit should contain obvious passwords.
- Every commit message should follow a standard.
- Code formatting should be checked.
- Tests should run before a commit is accepted.
Can Git automatically execute scripts during certain Git operations?

Yes.

This is possible using Git Hooks.
Definition
Git Hooks are scripts that Git executes automatically at specific points in its workflow.

Git stores sample hook files in:
.git/hooks/

Check:
ls .git/hooks

You may see:
applypatch-msg.sample
commit-msg.sample
post-update.sample
pre-commit.sample
pre-push.sample
prepare-commit-msg.sample

These are sample files.
Important Git Hooks

| Hook | When it executes |
| pre-commit | Before a commit is created |
| prepare-commit-msg | Before the commit message editor opens |
| commit-msg | After the message is prepared, before commit completes |
| post-commit | After a commit completes |
| pre-push | Before pushing to a remote |
| post-checkout | After checking out a branch or commit |
| post-merge | After a successful merge |

Practical 7 – Create a Pre-Commit Hook
We will create a simple hook that prevents commits containing a specific word in a file.

Initialize a repository:
mkdir git-hooks-lab
cd git-hooks-lab

git init

Navigate to:
.git/hooks/

Create a file named:
pre-commit

On Ubuntu/Git Bash:
nano .git/hooks/pre-commit

Add:
#!/bin/sh

if grep -R -n --exclude-dir=.git "PASSWORD=" .; then
    echo "ERROR: Potential hardcoded password found!"
    echo "Commit blocked."
    exit 1
fi

exit 0

Save it.

Make it executable:
chmod +x .git/hooks/pre-commit

Create a file:
echo "PASSWORD=admin123" > config.txt

Stage:
git add .

Try committing:
git commit -m "Added configuration"

The hook should block the commit.

Now remove the problematic content:
echo "APPLICATION=The Fairy Bake" > config.txt

Stage again:
git add config.txt

Commit:
git commit -m "Added application configuration"

This time the commit should succeed.
Important DevSecOps connection
Git Hooks can be used for local quality checks, but they are not a complete security boundary.

A developer can bypass local hooks.

For stronger enforcement, use server-side controls, protected branches and CI/CD security checks.

Also, this simple example is only a teaching demonstration, not a production-grade secret scanner.
PART 12 – Understanding Git Aliases
19. What is a Git Alias?
Suppose developers frequently execute:
git log --oneline --graph --all --decorate

Typing this repeatedly is inconvenient.

Git allows developers to create shortcuts called aliases.
Practical 8 – Create Git Aliases
Create an alias:
git config --global alias.st status

Now execute:
git st

Git executes:
git status

Create another:
git config --global alias.co checkout

Now:
git co main

Create a branch-switch alias:
git config --global alias.sw switch

Create a commit-history alias:
git config --global alias.lg "log --oneline --graph --all --decorate"

Now execute:
git lg

List configuration:
git config --global --list

Classroom question
Can Git aliases improve developer productivity?

Yes. They reduce repetitive typing and make common workflows faster.
PART 13 – Understanding Git Submodules
20. What is a Git Submodule?
Suppose you are developing a large application.

Your project depends on another Git repository.

For example:
ecommerce-project/
│
├── frontend/
├── backend/
└── common-library/

The common-library is maintained separately in another Git repository.

Instead of manually copying its code, you can include it as a Git Submodule.
Definition
A Git Submodule allows one Git repository to include another Git repository at a specific path and track a particular commit of that repository.
Real-life example
Your company has:
- Main application repository.
- Authentication library repository.
- Payment library repository.
The main application can reference the other repositories using submodules.
Practical 9 – Create a Submodule
First, create two repositories on GitHub:
common-library
main-application

Assume common-library contains:
common-library/
    common.py

Now clone the main application:
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/main-application.git
cd main-application

Add the common library:
git submodule add https://github.com/USERNAME/common-library.git common-library

Now check:
ls

You will see:
main-application/
│
├── common-library/
└── .gitmodules

The .gitmodules file contains submodule configuration.

Stage and commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Added common library submodule"
git push

How does another developer clone a project containing submodules?
Use:
git clone --recurse-submodules <repository-url>

If already cloned:
git submodule update --init --recursive

Important teaching point
A submodule does not simply copy the contents of another repository into the main repository.

It records a reference to a specific commit of that external repository.
PART 14 – Understanding Git LFS
21. What is Git LFS?
Git stands for Git Large File Storage.

Imagine your project contains:
project/
│
├── index.html
├── style.css
├── video.mp4
├── product-demo.mov
├── large-image.psd
└── design-assets.zip

Normal Git is optimized for tracking source-code changes, not repeatedly changing large binary files.

Large binary files can make repositories difficult to manage.

Git LFS helps manage these files.
Definition
Git LFS is an extension for Git that replaces large files in the repository with small pointer files while storing the actual file contents in a separate LFS storage system.

How does Git LFS work?
1. Developer installs Git LFS.
2. Developer identifies large file types to track.
3. Git LFS stores pointer files in Git history.
4. Actual large file contents are stored in LFS storage.
5. Git LFS retrieves the corresponding content when needed.
Practical 10 – Git LFS
First install Git LFS.

Official website:

https://git-lfs.com/

On Ubuntu, installation is available through the official Git LFS package instructions.

On Windows, download and install Git LFS.

Initialize:
git lfs install

Tell Git LFS to track MP4 files:
git lfs track "*.mp4"

Check:
cat .gitattributes

You should see a tracking rule similar to:
*.mp4 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text

Create or copy a video file:
demo.mp4

Stage:
git add .gitattributes demo.mp4

Commit:
git commit -m "Added demo video using Git LFS"

Push:
git push

Git LFS uploads the large content through the LFS mechanism, subject to the hosting provider's storage and bandwidth limits.
Why use Git LFS?
- Large video files.
- Design files.
- Game assets.
- Audio files.
- Large binary datasets.
Important: Git LFS has storage and bandwidth limits depending on the hosting plan. Students should understand that Git LFS does not mean unlimited storage.
